WEBVTT

00:00:08,850 --> 00:00:17,810
I recently had a screened mammogram and they want me to come back for a diagnostic mammogram

00:00:17,810 --> 00:00:24,130
and possibly an ultrasound. So usually if you have a screening mammogram that they found

00:00:24,130 --> 00:00:32,290
some lesions, so what an oncologist or a radiologist uses the term lesions, whether they're looking

00:00:32,290 --> 00:00:41,010
at an X-ray or a mammogram or a CT scan or an MRI, they'll use the word lesions to say that

00:00:41,010 --> 00:00:49,330
there's something going on that they saw abnormal on the scan. That doesn't mean a lesion like

00:00:49,330 --> 00:00:56,690
you think of, like it has to be a tumor or something like that, but there's something abnormal there.

00:00:56,690 --> 00:01:02,130
Certainly it could be a tumor, it could be cancer, but it suggests that we need to investigate

00:01:02,130 --> 00:01:07,330
a little bit deeper. Then they may want to do a deeper diagnostic ultrasound and there's some

00:01:07,330 --> 00:01:12,770
new type of 3D ultrasounds depending on where you're going that are 3D mammograms,

00:01:13,970 --> 00:01:20,050
depending on where you're going that could get a better picture. There's some brand new technology out

00:01:20,050 --> 00:01:24,690
that not very many places have that it's a totally different type of mammogram, which is actually

00:01:24,770 --> 00:01:33,090
pretty neat and excited for that to come on the market. Then from there, if they still see that

00:01:33,090 --> 00:01:41,650
this mass is suspicious, they may request to do an ultrasound guided biopsy. That's kind of the

00:01:41,650 --> 00:01:52,050
progression with breast issues, and that's fairly normal. Now if you ultimately do want to find out

00:01:52,050 --> 00:01:58,130
whether it's a cyst or it's a cancer, you have to get a biopsy. The only way to know

00:01:59,170 --> 00:02:05,170
that you absolutely know whether it's cancer or not is to get a pathology report. You could only do

00:02:05,170 --> 00:02:10,930
that through a biopsy. From a medical legal standpoint, you have to have a biopsy in order to have a

00:02:10,930 --> 00:02:18,450
diagnosis. In order to have a cancer diagnosis, it has to be done via pathology report via biopsy.

00:02:19,250 --> 00:02:25,410
This person goes on to ask about whether they can do a thermography. Is that helpful?

00:02:25,410 --> 00:02:31,490
So if thermography is very helpful to see if there is metabolic activity, because thermography is

00:02:31,490 --> 00:02:39,250
going to pick up heat, and you're going to have an increase in metabolic activity. That can be

00:02:39,250 --> 00:02:46,690
very helpful, but if thermography is not going to be diagnostic of cancer, nor is it diagnostic,

00:02:46,690 --> 00:02:53,570
that there's not cancer if the thermography is within normal limits. So a thermography is a

00:02:53,570 --> 00:03:00,530
screening tool that you have to understand that. So you wouldn't say, okay, I don't want to do a

00:03:00,530 --> 00:03:06,610
biopsy. Can I just use a tooth thermography? No. If you're free,

00:03:06,610 --> 00:03:14,130
a mammography, and you said, I don't want to do a mammography. I want to just do thermography.

00:03:15,490 --> 00:03:20,690
Then I would say, yeah, you can make those kind of decisions, those health care decisions. You don't

00:03:20,690 --> 00:03:28,770
want to do mammography because they are radiation. And use thermography as your screening tool,

00:03:28,770 --> 00:03:34,770
and then from that screening tool, go and do more comprehensive diagnostic tests like

00:03:34,770 --> 00:03:47,170
ultrasounds, and or further on with biopsies if the thermography was positive.

